      "excerpt": "No firmness is universally \"best\" for back pain; sleep position and body weight decide it. Here is what actually drives the choice, and why medium-firm is where most people start.",
      "directAnswer": "No single firmness is best for back pain. Sleep position and body weight both change the answer. A plush surface lets the hips settle in, which relieves pressure for side sleepers but can pull the spine out of line if it is too soft for a back or stomach sleeper. A firm surface holds the spine level for back and stomach sleeping, though it can leave side sleepers with pressure at the shoulder and hip. Medium-firm, the Amerisleep AS2 or AS3 for instance, is the starting point recommended most often for general back comfort.",
      "answersSI": "Plush and firm mattresses address different problems, and back pain has no one firmness that resolves it. A plush surface lets the hips and shoulders settle in, which spares side sleepers the pressure points they would otherwise feel, but if it gives too easily the lower back can curve out of neutral alignment — a particular concern for back and stomach sleepers. A firm surface holds the sleeper more level, which suits back and stomach sleeping, yet it can press into the shoulder and hip of a side sleeper, since there is less give to take up those bony points. Body weight moves the whole scale: a heavier body sinks further into the identical mattress, so a surface that reads as medium to one person reads as soft to another. Medium-firm is the most common general starting point, but position and weight tell you more than any single firmness number does.",
