          "heading": "What's Happening in the Body",
          "headingLevel": 2,
          "content": "The leading explanation for sleep pressure centers on adenosine, a compound that builds up in the brain during waking hours. As adenosine levels climb, so does the physical urge to sleep, which is part of why most people feel progressively more tired as the day goes on, peaking around their usual bedtime. Sleep clears out accumulated adenosine, so sleep pressure resets after a full night's rest and starts building again the next day. This system runs alongside the circadian rhythm but operates independently of it — sleep pressure builds regardless of the time of day, based purely on hours spent awake.",
