      "excerpt": "Nighttime overheating usually comes down to one of three causes: the mattress, the bedding, or the room. Identifying which one applies makes the fix far more effective than changing everything at once.",
      "directAnswer": "The first step in stopping nighttime overheating is identifying its source: a dense, older memory foam mattress that traps heat, synthetic or heavy bedding, or a room that runs too warm or lacks airflow. The right fix follows from the cause — breathable natural-fiber sheets, removing a heavy topper or comforter, lowering the room temperature or improving airflow, or switching to a hybrid or open-cell foam mattress if the mattress itself is the source.",
      "answersSI": "Nighttime overheating generally traces back to one of three sources, and the fix depends on which applies. When the mattress runs hot, dense, traditional memory foam is the usual culprit — its closed-cell structure holds body heat against the skin — so a hybrid with a coil base or an open-cell, plant-based foam mattress allows more air to move through. When the bedding is the issue, synthetic sheets and a heavy comforter trap heat that breathable cotton or linen and lighter layers would let escape. When the room itself is the problem, poor ventilation or an overnight thermostat set too high will overheat almost any sleep setup. Working through these three sources in order beats assuming the mattress is always to blame.",
