      "readTimeMinutes": 8,
      "wordCountTarget": 1500,
      "excerpt": "How to actually evaluate a mattress during its trial period — what to check in the fine print, how long to wait before judging it, and what to track each night.",
      "directAnswer": "Evaluate a mattress trial period by reading the fine print on trial length and return shipping costs before you buy, sleeping on the mattress for at least 30 nights since most foam needs a break-in period, tracking specific symptoms like back pain, overheating, or partner motion in a simple nightly log, and knowing the exact return process and deadline before you start the trial.",
      "answersSI": "To properly evaluate a mattress trial: check the trial length and who pays for return shipping before purchasing, give the mattress a minimum 30-night break-in period before judging it, log specific issues — back pain, sleeping hot, feeling your partner move — each night, and confirm exactly how and when to initiate a return well before the deadline arrives.",
      "citableFacts": [
        "Mattress trial periods commonly range from 90 to 365 nights depending on the brand, and the fine print on return shipping costs and restocking fees varies significantly between companies.",
        "Memory foam mattresses typically need a break-in period of around 30 nights for the foam to soften and for a sleeper's body to adjust to a new feel.",
        "Tracking specific, recurring symptoms — rather than relying on general impressions — gives a clearer signal of whether a mattress is actually working.",
        "Most mattress brands require the return process to be initiated before the trial period's deadline, even if pickup or shipping happens afterward."
      ],
      "entityMentions": [
        "Amerisleep AS3",
        "Amerisleep AS3"
      ],
      "schemaType": "HowTo",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": null,
          "headingLevel": null,
          "content": "A trial period only tells you anything if you use it deliberately, rather than assuming the answer will be obvious within the first week. Here is how to pull a real signal out of a trial period instead of just guessing.",
          "hasBulletList": false,
          "hasTable": false
        },
        {
          "heading": "What You Will Need",
          "headingLevel": 2,
          "content": "Evaluating a trial period well doesn't take special tools, just a bit of planning before the mattress arrives.",
          "hasBulletList": true,
          "hasTable": false,
          "bulletItems": [
            "The specific trial terms for that brand: length, return shipping cost, and any restocking fee, checked before you buy.",
            "An easy method for logging nightly notes, a notes app, a journal, or a recurring phone reminder all work.",
            "The patience to hold off on a final opinion for at least 30 nights.",
            "The precise return initiation steps and contact method (phone, online form, or email) kept somewhere you can find quickly."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Step 1: Read the Fine Print Before You Buy",
          "headingLevel": 3,
          "content": "Before purchasing, confirm the exact trial length in nights (not a vague \"100-night trial\" without specifics), who pays for return shipping or pickup if you decide to send it back, and whether a restocking fee applies. Some brands offer free returns; others deduct a fee or ask you to cover shipping a heavy, boxed mattress yourself. Knowing this upfront avoids an unpleasant surprise if the mattress doesn't work out.",
          "hasBulletList": false,
          "hasTable": false
        },
        {
          "heading": "Step 2: Give It at Least 30 Nights Before Judging",
          "headingLevel": 3,
          "content": "Most foam mattresses need a break-in period of around 30 nights: the foam softens slightly with regular use, and the body needs time to adjust to a new sleep surface, especially when switching from a very different firmness or material. Judging a mattress in the first week often reflects adjustment discomfort rather than a genuine mismatch. Plan to sleep on it through at least one full month before deciding.",
          "hasBulletList": false,
          "hasTable": false
        },
        {
          "heading": "Step 3: Track Specific Symptoms Each Night",
          "headingLevel": 3,
          "content": "Keep a simple nightly log instead of leaning on a vague overall impression. Note specifics: Did you wake up with back or hip pain? Did you sleep hot? Did you feel your partner moving more than usual? A short log kept over several weeks reveals patterns, a mattress that runs warm every single night, or one where back pain only shows up in certain sleep positions, that are easy to miss if you're relying on memory at the end of the trial.",
          "hasBulletList": false,
          "hasTable": false
        },
        {
          "heading": "Step 4: Know the Return Process Before the Deadline",
          "headingLevel": 3,
          "content": "Find out exactly how to start a return, phone call, online form, or email, and how many days before the trial deadline you need to initiate it. Many brands require the return to be started before the trial ends, even if the actual pickup or shipping happens afterward. Mark the deadline on a calendar a week or two early so you're not deciding under last-minute time pressure.",
          "hasBulletList": false,
          "hasTable": false
        },
        {
          "heading": "Mistakes to Avoid",
          "headingLevel": 2,
          "content": "This is where people most often end up either stuck with a mattress that isn't working or too late to return one that is:",
          "hasBulletList": true,
          "hasTable": false,
          "bulletItems": [
            "Forming a verdict during the first week, when early discomfort usually reflects adjustment rather than a real mismatch.",
            "Waiting until the trial's final day to start the return process once you've already decided to send it back.",
            "Leaning on a general impression instead of specific nightly notes, which catch patterns memory alone tends to miss.",
            "Skipping the return shipping terms before buying; a free trial and a free return aren't the same thing."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "faqs": [
        {
          "question": "How long should I wait before deciding on a new mattress?",
          "answer": "Hold off for a minimum of 30 nights. Most foam mattresses go through a break-in stretch, and the body takes time to adjust to a new feel, so a verdict formed in the first week or two usually reflects that adjustment rather than a genuine mismatch."
        },
        {
          "question": "Do I have to pay for return shipping during a mattress trial?",
          "answer": "That varies by brand. Some cover the full return, pickup included; others bill a shipping fee or a restocking fee. Check the specific terms before you buy so nothing catches you off guard if a return becomes necessary."
        },
        {
          "question": "What should I track during a mattress trial period?",
          "answer": "Specific, recurring issues tell you more than an overall impression does. Note morning back or hip pain, nighttime overheating, and how much you notice a partner's movement. A pattern across several weeks is a more reliable signal than any single night's reaction."
        },
        {
          "question": "What happens if I miss the trial period deadline?",
          "answer": "After the trial period closes, most brands stop accepting returns and the sale is treated as final. Note the deadline well in advance and begin the return process a few days early if you're still undecided."
        }
      ],
