      "wordCountTarget": 1500,
      "excerpt": "A look at what drives memory foam contouring — the density and response speed behind how closely foam follows your body, plus the trade-off between deep contouring and ease of movement.",
      "directAnswer": "Contouring describes how closely memory foam follows your body shape under pressure and body heat, and it's driven mainly by foam density and how quickly the foam responds. Slow-recovery memory foam contours deeply and isolates motion well but can feel like sinking in; faster-responding foams, like Bio-Pur, contour with less of a stuck-in-place sensation. Deeper contouring generally means better pressure relief but can trade off some ease of movement — a pattern that shows up across foam types.",
      "answersSI": "Contouring describes how closely memory foam follows your body shape under pressure and body heat, and it's driven mainly by foam density and how quickly the foam responds. Slow-recovery memory foam contours deeply and isolates motion well but can feel like sinking in; faster-responding foams, like Bio-Pur, contour with less of a stuck-in-place sensation. Deeper contouring generally means better pressure relief but can trade off some ease of movement — a pattern that shows up across foam types.",
      "citableFacts": [
        "Contouring is a measure of how closely foam traces the body's shape once pressure and body heat act on it.",
        "How deeply a foam contours comes down mainly to two factors: its density and how fast it responds.",
        "Traditional, slow-recovery viscoelastic foam contours deeply and isolates motion well, though it can produce a sinking sensation.",
        "Faster-response foams, Bio-Pur among them, are built to contour with less of a stuck or trapped sensation.",
        "The basic trade-off across foam types is that deeper contouring generally improves pressure relief while costing some ease of movement."
      ],
      "entityMentions": [
        "Amerisleep AS5",
        "Amerisleep AS3"
      ],
      "schemaType": "Article",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": null,
          "headingLevel": null,
          "content": "Contouring is the term mattress brands use for how closely a foam layer traces the shape of your body once you lie down. It's a real, measurable property of the foam itself, driven by density and response speed, and it's why two memory foam mattresses can feel entirely different even when both are marketed as \"pressure relieving.\"",
          "hasBulletList": false,
          "hasTable": false
        },
        {
          "heading": "What Density and Response Speed Do",
          "headingLevel": 2,
          "content": "Memory foam contours by softening in response to pressure and body heat, then gradually returning to its original shape once that pressure lifts. How deeply it contours, and how it feels while doing so, comes down to two factors: density (denser foam tends to contour more closely and hold up longer over time) and response speed (how fast the foam softens and how fast it springs back). Traditional, slow-recovery viscoelastic memory foam softens and conforms deeply, which is why it isolates motion so well — a partner shifting on their side of the bed barely registers on yours. But that same slow response produces the classic \"sinking in\" feeling, where changing position takes a beat of effort. Faster-responding foams, like Amerisleep's Bio-Pur blend, are engineered to soften and rebound more quickly, which still delivers contouring and pressure relief but reduces the sense of being pinned in one spot.",
          "hasBulletList": false,
          "hasTable": false
        },
        {
          "heading": "What This Means in Practice",
          "headingLevel": 2,
          "content": "The practical takeaway is that more contouring isn't automatically better — it's a trade-off, and which side of it suits you depends on what you're optimizing for.",
          "hasBulletList": true,
          "hasTable": false,
          "bulletItems": [
            "Deeper, slower contouring tends to relieve pressure points more thoroughly and isolates motion well, which benefits side sleepers and couples on different sleep schedules.",
            "Deeper contouring can make position changes feel more effortful, since the foam takes a moment to release and reform.",
            "Faster-response foams contour with less of a stuck-in-place feeling, which can suit combination sleepers who shift positions often.",
            "Foam density also affects durability, not just feel — denser foams generally hold their contouring ability longer than lower-density foams."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "How to Use This When Comparing Mattresses",
          "headingLevel": 2,
          "content": "When comparing memory foam mattresses, ask what type of foam sits in the comfort layer and whether it's described as slow-recovery or fast-response, since that distinction predicts the contouring trade-off more reliably than the firmness rating alone. Amerisleep's AS5, for instance, uses a thicker comfort layer of Bio-Pur foam aimed at deep contouring for side sleepers while keeping the faster-response feel; the AS3 uses a similar foam in a more moderate layer for sleepers who want some contouring without as much sink. Whichever you're considering, the right amount of contouring comes down to whether you value pressure relief or ease of movement more for your own sleep style.",
          "hasBulletList": false,
          "hasTable": false
        }
      ],
      "faqs": [
        {
          "question": "What controls how much a memory foam mattress contours?",
          "answer": "It comes down mostly to foam density and how fast the foam reacts to pressure and heat. Denser, slower-recovery foam tends to contour more deeply, while lighter, faster-response foam contours with less of a sinking sensation."
        },
        {
          "question": "Is more contouring always the better choice?",
          "answer": "Not always. Deeper contouring tends to improve pressure relief and motion isolation, but it can also make moving or changing positions feel more effortful. It's a trade-off, not a straightforward upgrade."
        },
        {
          "question": "How does traditional memory foam differ from Bio-Pur foam?",
          "answer": "Traditional viscoelastic memory foam is typically slower to respond, contouring deeply but with a more pronounced sinking feeling. Bio-Pur is a faster-response foam designed to contour with less of that stuck-in-place sensation."
        },
        {
          "question": "Who benefits most from deep contouring?",
          "answer": "Side sleepers and people sensitive to a partner's movement tend to benefit most from deeper, slower contouring, since it relieves pressure points and isolates motion well. Combination sleepers who shift positions often may prefer a faster-response foam instead. See our full scored comparisons at /best/overall/ for foam type breakdowns across scored mattresses."
        }
      ],
