          "headingLevel": 2,
          "content": "Pain signals can trigger brief arousals, partial awakenings that interrupt the normal progression through sleep stages, even when a person doesn't fully wake up or remember it later. Repeated arousals tend to reduce the proportion of time spent in deep sleep, the stage most associated with physical restoration. Over time, that fragmented pattern connects to research showing that poor or shortened sleep can lower the threshold for pain the following day, meaning the same stimulus can feel more painful after a bad night. That creates a feedback loop: pain disrupts sleep, disrupted sleep amplifies pain, and amplified pain disrupts sleep further. Mattress and pressure-related factors are one input into this cycle, alongside the underlying pain condition itself.",
